                        p.abstract__synopsis
                            | Little is known about the underlying mechanisms that generate the perception of skin wetness.
                            | Humans have no specific hygroreceptor, and thus temperature and touch information combine to produce wetness sensations.
                            | The present review covers the potential mechanisms leading to the perception of wetness,
                            | both peripherally and centrally, along with their implications for manual function.
                            | These insights are relevant to inform the design of neuroengineering interfaces, such as sensory prostheses for amputees.
